<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">BASF</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">BASF is a German chemical company founded in 1865. It became one of the world's leading producers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/synthetic-dye">synthetic dyes</a> and later developed the industrial synthesis of ammonia. It was an example of the strength of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-empire">German Empire</a>'s chemical industry and science-based industrialisation.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/basf.jpg" alt="BASF" width="320" height="116" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: The original uploader was Afrank99 at German Wikipedia.: we create logos · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:BASF-Logo_bw.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>BASF is an acronym for Badische Anilin- und Sodafabrik, the Baden Aniline and Soda Factory. It was founded by Friedrich Engelhorn on 6 April 1865 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mannheim">Mannheim</a>, in the state of Baden. Engelhorn had set up a gasworks and street lighting for the town council in 1861, and used the tar it produced as a by-product of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coal">coal</a> to extract aniline for dyes. The plant was built across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> at Ludwigshafen because the Mannheim council feared air pollution, and dye production moved there in 1866.</p><p>William Henry Perkin's discovery in 1857 that aniline could make intense colouring agents had begun the commercial production of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/synthetic-dye">synthetic dyes</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>. BASF recruited the chemist Heinrich Caro as its first head of research. He developed a synthesis for the red dye alizarin and applied for a British patent on 25 June 1869, one day before Perkin applied for a virtually identical one; the two companies reached a commercial agreement. A synthetic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indigo">indigo</a> reached the market in 1897, and in 1901 about 80% of production was dyestuffs.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Other processes followed. In 1890 a unit using the contact process produced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sulfuric-acid">sulfuric acid</a> at 98% concentration, the result of research by Rudolf Knietsch. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haber-process">Haber process</a>, developed from 1908 to 1912, made it possible to synthesise ammonia, BASF acquired exclusive rights and in 1913 opened a plant at Oppau, adding fertilizers. In 1916 it opened a site at Leuna where explosives were made during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>. An explosion at Oppau on 21 September 1921 killed 565 people, the biggest industrial accident in German history.</p><p>Under Carl Bosch, BASF founded IG Farben with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hoechst">Hoechst</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayer">Bayer</a> and three other companies, losing its independence. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adolf-hitler">Adolf Hitler</a> became chancellor in 1933, IG Farben cooperated with the National Socialist government and profited from forced labour; by 1943 nearly half its workers were forced labourers, besides <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/concentration-camp">concentration camp</a> prisoners supplied by the Nazis. IG Farben built a large chemical factory at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oswiecim">Auschwitz</a> and produced Zyklon B, the gas used to kill prisoners in the extermination camps. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allies">Allies</a> dissolved IG Farben in November 1945, and the Ludwigshafen site, bombed 65 times, had been almost completely destroyed.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>An explosion at the Ludwigshafen site on 28 July 1948 killed 207 people. BASF was refounded under its name in 1952 and shared in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wirtschaftswunder">German economic miracle</a> of the 1950s, adding synthetics such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nylon">nylon</a>, and it invented Styropor in 1951. After a change of strategy in 1965 it put more weight on coatings, pharmaceuticals, pesticides and fertilizers, and it expanded production abroad.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “BASF” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>By 1913 German chemical firms such as <b>BASF</b>, Bayer and Hoechst supplied roughly 80 to 90 per cent of the world's synthetic dyes.</blockquote><a href="/empire/german-empire">German Empire · At a glance</a></li></ul></section>
